{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Replace six verbs with one noun and a coherent verb set.\n\n    rebar bridge setup | check-access | fsck | status | preview | sync | pause | resume\n\nThree verbs retire. `reconcile` goes with NO alias, because the defaults invert: `rebar reconcile`\nwith no arguments means dry-run (the CLI wrapper injects `--mode dry-run` at\n`_cli/__init__.py:145-160`, while `python -m rebar_reconciler` itself defaults to LIVE, and\n`_lib_ops.py:92` defaults to dry-run again — three entry points, two conventions). `bridge sync`\nwith no arguments mutates. Aliasing would be a silent behaviour change; anyone with `reconcile` in\na script must re-read the line.\n\n`bridge-status` retires because its producer has never existed in this repo — rebar was extracted\nfrom digital-service-orchestra on 2026-06-08 and the reader was a knowing tombstone from day one.\nNote the citation in its docstring, \"epic 3a03\", is DANGLING: in this repo 3a03 resolves to an\nunrelated test-isolation bug created 11 days AFTER the comment citing it. The real epic is\n`3006-e198-13db-4e1f`.\n\n`purge-bridge` retires: five commits, every one an automated lint sweep or safety retrofit, none a\nfeature or bug fix, and no script, workflow, or runbook invokes it. Its `--keep` flag is also\ninverted-sense (it deletes everything NOT matching).\n\n`--mode` disappears entirely. Its five values collapse to `preview`/`sync` plus `--max-changes N`;\nthe two `bootstrap-*` values carry no semantics beyond their cap integer (the only non-applier\nconsumers test `MODE_CAPS[m] != 0` and never read 10 or 100).\n\nFlags survive only if their help text can be written without internals. Ten candidates reduced to\nthree: `--max-changes N`, `--only <ids>`, `--except <ids>`, `--max-age <duration>`, plus the house\n`--output json`. Maintainer decision: `--only`/`--except` limit what is EXAMINED as well as what is\nchanged (unlike the internal `--filter-local-ids` they replace, which filtered only writes and was\nmarked \"for validation use only\" for that reason), and preview/sync remains the read/write knob.\n\n`check-access` is its own verb, not a flag on the health verb: all three narrowing-flag precedents\n(`flux check --pre`, `dbt debug --connection`, `gh auth status`) are READ-ONLY, and none mutates the\nremote — so the precedent does not transfer to an operation that creates and deletes a real Jira\nissue. `fsck` audits your records; `check-access` proves your access.\n\n`setup` calls `check-access` as its final validation step.\n\n## Review remediation (2026-08-06) — consumers, verb semantics, cap-0 split\n\n**B3 — The production consumer is enumerated and migrated.**\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:262` runs `rebar reconcile --mode \"$MODE\"`. This is not an\nincidental caller: `$MODE` is the documented ROLLBACK BRAKE (epic `3006-e198-13db-4e1f`), so retiring\n`--mode` removes an operational safety control unless its replacement is stated. Mapping:\n* `MODE=reconcile-check` / `MODE=dry-run` -> `bridge preview` (no mutation)\n* `MODE=bootstrap-strict` / `bootstrap-throttle` -> `bridge sync --max-changes 10` / `--max-changes 100`\n* `MODE=live` -> `bridge sync` (uncapped)\n* the brake's \"stop entirely\" position is no longer a mode at all — it is `bridge pause` (sibling\n  `1104`), which is strictly stronger because it is remote-persisted and applies fleet-wide rather\n  than to one workflow_dispatch invocation.\nThe `workflow_dispatch` input is renamed accordingly in the same change, so the brake is never absent.\n\n**B4 — The two cap-0 modes are behaviourally distinct, and both land in `bridge preview`.**\nThe collapse rationale covers the bootstrap-* cap integers but not the cap-0 pair, which are NOT the\nsame operation: `reconcile-check` takes NO lock, returns early at `__main__.py:577`, and emits a\nbound-pair discrepancy report; `dry-run` takes the lock, runs the full differ pass with\n`persist=False`, and writes a manifest. Neither output is derivable from the other. Sibling `424f`\nowns the merge and is explicit that this is real work, not a rename: ONE verb, ONE pass, two sections\n`{plan, drift}`, with `_compare_pair` DELETED and field detail sourced from the differ. This ticket\ntherefore retires the `--mode` spellings only; `424f` owns the behavioural merge, and `424f`\ndepends_on this ticket.\n\n**B6 — User-facing strings that name a retired verb are enumerated.**\nRetiring `bridge-status` leaves two strings pointing at a verb that no longer exists:\n`_engine_support/bridge.py:32` (`_USAGE = \"Usage: ticket bridge-status [--output json]\"`) and\n`src/rebar/schemas/bridge_status.schema.json:5` (whose description names\n`rebar bridge-status --output json`). Both are removed with the verb — the schema file is retired\noutright, since sibling `b69c` builds status on durable state and publishes its own schema.\n\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar bridge <verb>` exists with the agreed verb set\n- [ ] `reconcile`, `bridge-status`, `purge-bridge` are removed, with release notes; no aliases\n- [ ] `--mode` is gone from the CLI and the engine entrypoint (`_cli/__init__.py:145-160`,\n      `rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:501-508`, `mode.py`)\n- [ ] **Surface scope is explicit (review remediation).** `reconcile`/`mode` lives on THREE surfaces,\n      not one: the CLI, the public library API `rebar.reconcile(mode=...)` (`src/rebar/__init__.py:51`\n      re-exporting `_lib_ops.py:92`), and the MCP tool `reconcile(mode=...)` (`_mcp_reads.py:279`,\n      surfaced via `mcp_server.py`). THIS ticket retires the CLI + engine only. The library and MCP\n      retirements belong to sibling `f789`, which ADDS the bridge surface to those same two\n      interfaces — so each surface's old verb is removed by the same change that ships its\n      replacement, leaving no window where an MCP or library caller has neither. `f789` already\n      depends_on this ticket, which enforces that order.\n- [ ] A test asserts the CLI no longer accepts `--mode` or the `reconcile` verb, AND that the library\n      and MCP entrypoints are still callable at this ticket's boundary (proving the staged retirement\n      left no surface broken mid-epic)\n- [ ] Every shipped flag's help text contains no internals (no \"mode\", \"rank\", \"gate\", \"lock\", \"cap\", \"pass\", \"binding store\", no exit-code numbers)\n- [ ] `bridge` appears in `rebar --help`. Today `reconcile` appears ZERO times while FOUR\n      bridge-prefixed verbs are listed — `purge-bridge`, `bridge-status`, `bridge-fsck`,\n      `bridge-probe` — two of which (`purge-bridge`, `bridge-status`) are dead. Verified against\n      `rebar --help` on 2026-08-06\n- [ ] The dangling \"epic 3a03\" citation is corrected wherever it survives\n- [ ] Each new verb is proven to FUNCTION, not merely to exist (B1): `preview` makes no Jira write and\n      no ticket-event write (asserted against a store snapshot taken before and after); `sync` mutates;\n      `pause` then `status` reports paused and a subsequent `sync` declines; `resume` restores it\n- [ ] `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:262` is migrated off `rebar reconcile --mode \"$MODE\"`,\n      and its `workflow_dispatch` mode input is renamed to the new vocabulary in the SAME change, so\n      the epic-3006 rollback brake is never absent (B3)\n- [ ] `_engine_support/bridge.py:32`'s `_USAGE` string and `schemas/bridge_status.schema.json` are\n      removed with the `bridge-status` verb; a test greps the shipped tree for the retired verb names\n      and fails if any user-facing string still names one (B6)\n- [ ] The health verb is `fsck` everywhere — `doctor` is not shipped, not as an alias, and appears\n      nowhere in help, docs or code (B2/B7)\n- [ ] `check-access` is in the shipped verb set and `setup` calls it as its final validation step"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786085660378706001,"uuid":"88c8244f-8e10-40cc-a5dc-ec29a5b368d8"}